      'Trading of Government/Agency Debt',
      'Trading of government and agency securities takes place solely in the over-the-counter market and not on an exchange. The participants in this over-the-counter (OTC) market include large commercial banks, foreign banks, U.S. government securities dealers, and full-service brokerage firms, as well as the Federal Reserve (the Fed) itself. The largest participants in the market are the primary U.S. government securities dealers.\n\nPrimary dealers are hooked up to the Federal Reserve wire system and deal directly with the Fed.\n\nAll other firms trading U.S. government securities are termed secondary dealers. These firms buy and sell Treasuries in the market through the primary dealers and can also bid in the weekly auction. Most smaller banks and brokerage firms are secondary dealers.\n\nOpen market operations involve the Fed engaging in the trading of U.S. Treasury securities. The Fed maintains its \'own trading account that buys and sells large quantities of government securities in the market to manage interest rates.\n\nWhen the Fed wishes to loosen credit, it will buy Treasury securities from the primary dealers. The primary dealers then provide cash to the other dealers, most of whom are banks. Market interest rates are lowered since the banks now have more cash to lend.\n\nThis would be considered a dovish stance by the Fed designed to spur economic growth.\n\nConversely, when the Fed wishes to tighten credit, it will sell Treasury securities to the primary dealers. This action removes cash from the banking system. Market interest rates will go up since banks have less cash to lend.\n\nThis would be considered a hawkish stance by the Fed designed to slow economic growth. The market for agency securities is dominated by the primary dealers. U.S. government issues are initially sold by competitive bid at a weekly yield auction.\n\nAgency securities are issued differently. Each agency has assembled a selling group of firms that sell the agency\'s issues. These firms act as fiscal agents for the agency.\n\nMost agency securities are quoted by dealers on a yield spread basis against equivalent maturing Treasuries. Example Typically, Ginnie Mae bonds (GNMA bonds) trade with a yield spread of 25 to 50 basis points (bps) over Treasuries. So, if Treasury notes are yielding 5.00%, a dealer might price a GNMA bond to yield 5.25% (a 25- basis point yield spread over the Treasury).\n\nRegular way settlement for trades of most securities, including U.S. government debt (Treasury bills, notes, and bonds), corporate bonds, municipal securities, and mortgage-backed securities is one business day after the trade date (T+1).\n\nTrades of U.S. government and agency securities clear through the Federal Reserve wire system, known as the Fedwire Funds Service. These trades settle in fed funds. Fed funds are immediately payable at a Federal Reserve Branch member institution.',
